+# Decision: Delivery transport for committed changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event and assigns each committed edit one event revision. Committed revisions must reach connected browsers so staff see a current board. The transport that carries revisions from the server to browsers is a project-level choice: it shapes the change store (what it must retain and publish), delivery (what sessions or responses it owns), and browser sync (how browsers subscribe, detect loss, and catch up). Those three nodes are sized around this choice, so getting it wrong means re-cutting the boundaries between them.
+
+All candidate transports can meet the board's freshness goal — committed changes visible within 15 seconds under normal connectivity — at the required scale of 800 simultaneous browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. The choice is therefore not about feasibility but about which secondary costs the project is willing to own.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminates a candidate: every option can meet the freshness, scale, and 20-minute recovery goals, so those are background, not decision drivers. The choice turns entirely on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Update latency** (moderate). How quickly a committed revision reaches browsers. Subsecond updates are useful but not required; the floor is the 15-second freshness goal, which all options clear. Lower latency has standing value during opening-time bursts.
+- **Vendor independence** (major). Whether the board's delivery depends on a third-party realtime provider's subscription model, pricing, and availability. The project prefers not to couple a core path to a vendor.
+- **Recurring operating cost** (major). Ongoing spend attributable specifically to the delivery route, beyond the compute the rest of the stack already runs.
+- **Operational burden** (moderate). Connection capacity, session management, reliable publication, and browser reconnect/catch-up logic the project must build and operate itself.
+- **Change-store read load** (minor). Repeated read pressure the transport imposes on the change store to serve the current board.
+
+No current tenet leans this decision: the tenet *"Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted"* explicitly allows either direction, so the weighting of vendor independence and recurring cost above operational burden is a project preference exercised within that permission, not a tenet applied. (See Selection.)
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll a read endpoint on a fixed interval (about five seconds), passing a cursor so each response returns only revisions committed since the browser's last-seen revision. Delivery stays ordinary request/response; the change store exposes cursor-ordered read queries; browsers run a polling loop and advance their cursor. No long-lived connections exist.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **weak** — a committed change waits up to one poll interval plus a request round-trip, so browsers lag committed state by several seconds. Within the 15-second goal, but the least fresh of the three.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no third-party provider is involved.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no vendor fees; cost is the project's own compute.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless request/response with no connection state to manage; the browser loop and cursor advance are simple, and there is no reconnect machinery because there is no persistent connection.
+- Change-store read load: **weak** — 800 browsers re-reading every five seconds is sustained, largely redundant read traffic that the store must serve through cursor queries regardless of whether anything changed.
+
+**Summary.** Lowest operational burden, at the cost of the freshest updates and the highest steady read load.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The project operates its own WebSocket server. Browsers hold a persistent connection; when the server commits a revision it publishes that revision to connected browsers over their sockets. The change store retains replayable revisions and reliably hands committed revisions to a publication path; delivery manages socket sessions and the publication fan-out; browsers subscribe, detect connection loss, and request the revisions they missed on reconnect.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — revisions are pushed as they commit, with no polling interval between commit and delivery.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — delivery is operated entirely by the project; no realtime provider is in the path.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no vendor fees; the connection infrastructure is the project's own compute, the same cost class as the rest of the stack.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity for up to 800 concurrent sockets, session management, reliable publication, and the browser-side reconnect-and-catch-up logic that restores a board after up to 20 minutes offline.
+- Change-store read load: **strong** — browsers do not re-poll; current state is pushed, and the store serves bounded catch-up reads only on reconnect.
+
+**Summary.** Fast updates with no vendor dependency, paid for in owned connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider carries revisions to browsers. The server publishes committed revisions to the provider; browsers subscribe through the provider's client SDK. Delivery wraps the provider's publish API and manages provider credentials; the change store reconciles its revisions against what the provider has acknowledged as published; browsers use the provider's subscription and reconnect model.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — low-latency delivery is the provider's core product.
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — the board's delivery path is coupled to one provider's subscription model, pricing, and availability.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring per-connection or per-message fees scale with 800 browsers and burst traffic.
+- Operational burden: **partial** — the provider absorbs raw connection scaling and much reconnect handling, but the project still owns publication reconciliation against the provider and provider-credential management.
+- Change-store read load: **strong** — delivery is publication-based, so browsers do not impose polling reads on the store.
+
+**Summary.** The least connection operations and strong latency, paid for in recurring fees and coupling to a provider.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It is the only option strong on both major criteria at once. Against **vendor independence**, it keeps a core path under the project's own operation; the hosted service is weak here, and that alone makes the hosted service hard to accept for a path this central. Against **recurring operating cost**, it adds no vendor fees, where the hosted service's fees scale with browser count and bursts. It also takes the **update-latency** criterion that polling cannot — pushing revisions as they commit rather than on a five-second interval — and spares the change store the sustained redundant **read load** that 800 polling browsers would impose.
+
+The one criterion it loses is **operational burden** (moderate): the project must build and run connection capacity for up to 800 sockets, session management, reliable publication, and browser reconnect/catch-up for disconnections of up to 20 minutes. This cost is accepted deliberately in exchange for fast updates without a vendor dependency. Polling would avoid that burden but gives up latency and adds read load; the hosted service would reduce the burden partway but loses both major criteria.
+
+This decision is **not dictated by a tenet**. The tenet *"Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted"* permits either direction; the project here exercises a preference — vendor independence and bounded recurring cost over lower operational burden — that the tenet set does not itself encode.
+
+**What it forecloses and obligates.** Committing to self-hosted WebSockets obligates the three downstream nodes concretely: the change store must retain replayable revisions and publish committed revisions reliably; delivery must manage socket sessions and the publication fan-out; browser sync must detect connection loss, surface it (not mistake it for an unchanged board), and catch up on missed revisions after reconnect. It forecloses leaning on a provider to absorb connection scaling — the capacity plan for 800 sockets and opening-time bursts is now owned work.
