 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections the project operates itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected as adding repeated read load and a few seconds of update lag without reducing the overall work enough to justify the slower board. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring provider fees and coupling the board to a vendor's subscription model. The accepted trade-off is that connection capacity and browser reconnect/catch-up become owned operational work. Full reasoning: `docs/decisions/delivery-transport.md`.
